---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About">
	<main class="wrap">
		<h1>About this queue</h1>
		<p class="lead">
			A single-person bass practice queue: the songs I already know, kept fresh, plus the ones I'm learning now.
		</p>

		<div class="card">
			<h2>Where it came from</h2>
			<p>
				This started as a seven-week sprint to a reunion gig with the G6 band on
				<strong>October 9, 2026 at Canyon Lake</strong>. That gig is done, so the course
				has been retooled into a standing queue rather than a one-shot countdown.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="card">
			<h2>How the queue works</h2>
			<p>
				Each song has a <strong>status</strong> — <strong>cold</strong> (not started),
				<strong>dusty</strong> (rusty), <strong>warm</strong> (needs reps), or
				<strong>green</strong> (solid: roots and groove in time from memory, plus the
				recognizable hooks — fills are stretch, not required).
			</p>
			<p>
				Separately, a song can be <strong>archived</strong>. Archiving shelves a song
				without destroying its status: unarchive it and it comes back exactly where it
				was. Archived songs drop out of the queue, the status counts, and the progress
				meter, so the dashboard always answers "how is my active queue doing."
			</p>
			<p>
				"What now" points at the lowest-status song still in rotation, breaking ties by
				the order songs are listed. Nothing archived ever surfaces as work.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="card">
			<h2>How it's built</h2>
			<p>
				Course content is authored in Markdown/HTML under the hood, then served as a
				static site via GitHub Pages. The dashboard uses a small bit of client-side
				script to track song status and archive state in your own browser — nothing
				leaves the device. The trade-off: clearing site data resets the queue.
			</p>
		</div>
	</main>
</Layout>
